    citations: [],
    body: `<p>A net-worth tracking app's value depends on one specific thing — how accurately and automatically it aggregates every account you actually hold, not just its dashboard's visual polish.</p><h2>The Criteria to Evaluate</h2><p>Check broad account-type support — checking, savings, investment, retirement, and real estate/property value estimates all need to connect for a complete net worth picture, not just bank accounts alone. Verify secure read-only connections (via a recognized aggregator) for every linked account. Confirm the app tracks net worth as a trend over time, not just a single current snapshot — the trend is the more useful data point. And check whether manual asset entry (for accounts that can't auto-connect) is supported.</p><div class="callout callout-tip"><p><strong>Practically, this means:</strong> A net worth app that can't connect to every account type you hold (a specific retirement plan, a real estate value estimate, a less common bank) still has value if it supports accurate manual entry for those gaps — don't dismiss an otherwise strong app over one unsupported connection when manual entry can fill the gap.</p></div><div class="callout callout-info"><p><strong>Someone With Accounts an App Can't Auto-Connect:</strong> Check for robust manual entry support rather than dismissing the app entirely.</p></div><div class="callout callout-info"><p><strong>Someone New to Tracking Net Worth:</strong> Prioritize trend tracking over time, not just a single current snapshot.</p></div><h2>Evaluate Net Worth Apps the Way</h2><ol><li>Check broad account-type support across your actual holdings.</li><li>Verify secure, read-only connections for every linked account.</li><li>Prioritize trend tracking over a single snapshot view.</li></ol><p>See <a href="/personal-finance/personal-net-worth-calculator-guide">the personal net worth calculator guide</a> for the underlying calculation this tracking supports.</p>`,
