    body: `<p>Global trade flows directly affect corporate earnings and broader GDP data — a concrete and currently visible dynamic in the most recent economic reports, not an abstract concept.</p><h2>The Current Example</h2><p>The Q2 2026 GDP report showed net international trade subtracting a full <strong>1.0 percentage point</strong> from growth, as imports remained strong amid businesses adapting supply chains to a more permanent tariff landscape — a direct, currently-measurable example of trade policy affecting headline economic data. Companies with significant international supply chain exposure feel tariff and trade policy changes directly in their cost structure, which flows through to reported earnings and, ultimately, stock price reactions.</p><div class="callout callout-tip"><p>Checking a specific company's disclosed geographic revenue and supply chain exposure (typically detailed in the 10-K) reveals how directly trade policy changes might affect that company specifically — a domestically-focused company faces different trade-policy risk than one with heavy international manufacturing dependency.</p></div><div class="callout callout-info"><p><strong>Someone Evaluating a Company's Trade Policy Exposure:</strong> Check its disclosed geographic revenue and supply chain detail in the 10-K.</p></div><div class="callout callout-info"><p><strong>Someone Reading GDP Data Showing a Trade Drag:</strong> Recognize this specific and currently-documented dynamic (as in Q2 2026) rather than an abstract concept.</p></div><h2>Understand Trade's Market Impact the Way</h2><ol><li>Use the Q2 2026 net-trade drag as a concrete current example.</li><li>Check a specific company's supply chain exposure before assuming impact.</li><li>Watch GDP trade components alongside the headline growth number.</li></ol><p>See <a href="/gdp/gdp-growth-q2-2026">the Q2 2026 GDP growth report</a> for the fuller underlying data.</p>`,
